<LabLayout title="T4 experiment: model & assumptions · Tether Lab" description="The coupled motion, pivot loads, release continuity and limits behind the two-tier tether experiment." active="method">
  <main class="guide" id="lab-content">
    <header class="guide-header"><p class="micro">TWO-TIER TETHER / T4p-0.1.0</p><h1>Two stages share one motion.</h1><p>A small rotor turns on the end of a larger arm. The station, moving pivot and payload all respond to the same gravity and constraints.</p><div class="guide-meta"><span>FINITE MASSES</span><span>PASSIVE IDEAL HINGE</span><span>ONE TIMED RELEASE</span></div></header>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>The topology, and our approximation</h2><p>Tillotson’s two-tier tether appears in <a href="https://www.niac.usra.edu/files/studies/final_report/355Bogar.pdf#page=101">HASTOL Phase I, p. 19 and Appendix 2 A2-15–17</a>: a primary arm carries a pivot and a smaller two-arm rotor. This experiment explores that arrangement with uniform rigid stages and a finite station. It does not reproduce the historical sizing or mass-benefit claims.</p><p>The secondary rotates freely relative to the primary. An ideal planar hinge takes the place of the physical split end and bearing. <strong>Crossing clearance, bearing dimensions and flexible cable behavior are not modeled.</strong></p><div class="scope-grid"><div class="scope-box"><h3>Calculated</h3><ul><li>Both stage angles and the moving pivot trajectory</li><li>Distributed Earth gravity with finite station and cable masses</li><li>A pre-attached payload and impulse-free release</li><li>Axial stress, transverse constraint force and pivot force</li><li>Whole-segment clearance and energy/momentum checks</li></ul></div><div class="scope-box omitted"><h3>Outside this experiment</h3><ul><li>Atmospheric pickup, incoming capture and guidance</li><li>Cable flexure, vibration, bending strength and fatigue</li><li>Fork geometry, physical crossings and bearing qualification</li><li>Motors, active phase control and orbit/spin recovery</li><li>Repeated service or a completed launch system</li></ul></div></div></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>A moving pivot, not a prescribed path</h2><p>The primary spans 300 km from a 120 t station to a 6 t pivot. The secondary has two 50 km arms, each ending in a permanent 2 t terminal. Default cargo is 3 t on the positive secondary tip. All masses and dimensions are scenario choices.</p><p>The loaded center of mass starts at 1,800 km altitude with circular-orbit translation. Primary and secondary initial angular rates are specified as ω × arm length: 1 and 0.8 km/s by default. These are not ground speeds. Initial phase sets the angle between the stages; a negative secondary rate reverses its rotation.</p><p>At each step, gravity acts on the station, hardware, cargo and distributed cable mass. A coupled mass matrix determines pivot acceleration and both angular accelerations. Neither stage is forced to keep its original rate. No motor supplies energy.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Release keeps the existing velocity</h2><p>The cargo starts already attached. At the selected time, it separates without a push. Stage positions and velocities remain continuous; the detached mass then follows spherical-Earth gravity independently.</p><p>The release velocity is system translation plus two rotational vectors. The Flight Studio shows their X and Y components because their directions determine whether they reinforce or oppose each other. Adding their magnitudes would overstate the result.</p><p>The report checks total energy and angular momentum throughout the flight, including detached cargo, and checks both plus linear momentum at separation. Orbital/spin energy is part of the initial state; there is no reboost or free replenishment.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Loads are a screen, not a flight qualification</h2><p>The uniform Zylon HM and Kevlar 49 cables use the lab’s <a href="/lab/method/#materials">published fiber profiles</a>, divided by your safety factor. The calculation accumulates force along each arm, including cable mass and terminal loads. It reports peak axial stress and the force the secondary transmits through its pivot.</p><p>Rigid straight stages also require transverse forces. Those are shown alongside the pivot force; passing the axial check does not prove that a flexible cable remains straight or that a real bearing survives. Bending stress is not calculated.</p><p>The run stops on excessive axial stress, compression beyond a 1 N numerical tolerance, or any part of a stage or released cargo crossing <strong>120 km above Earth</strong>. This is an exclusion boundary, not an atmosphere model. The clear-flight check covers the displayed two hours only.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Try the phase challenge</h2><p>Keep the default hardware and release at two minutes. Starting at 60° gives about 3,040 km apoapsis; 180° gives about 9,500 km. Try to reach 8,000–12,000 km while keeping periapsis above 120 km and completing the model checks. “Compare six phases” calculates six complete flights with your current hardware and timing.</p><p>The brief’s periapsis and apoapsis describe the cargo’s Earth orbit at release. Changing cable area also changes its mass and the coupled motion. A thinner cable is not a free mass reduction.</p><p>T4 designs, JSON files and shared links have their own model version and browser storage. This experiment does not modify Earth, Moon, Phobos or Expeditions saves. Continue your existing campaign when you return.</p><a class="button-link primary" href="/lab/t4/">Open the T4 experiment →</a></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Compare release timing</h2><p>“Map release timing” crosses six initial phases with up to five release delays around your chosen time. Select 30-second, one-minute or two-minute spacing. Times stay within 0–30 minutes; duplicate rows at the boundaries are removed. Each cell runs the same full model as an ordinary flight.</p><p>Results appear as calculations finish. Select a cell to inspect its orbit, axial margin, pivot force and stop reason. The suggestion uses only passing samples, closest to 10,000 km apoapsis. <strong>The gaps between samples are untested.</strong> A passing cell does not establish a continuous release window or a timing tolerance.</p><p>Stopping a study keeps its completed samples and leaves your current flight intact. Opening a sample restores all its settings and reruns the flight. Changing fixed hardware marks an earlier study as out of date. Completed studies can export their inputs and results as JSON; studies are kept only for the current page session.</p></section>
    <section class="guide-section"><h2>Numerical resolution</h2><p>Uniform cable mass is integrated with two-point Gauss quadrature in 32 cells per arm. The same mass nodes supply gravity, potential energy and loads. A fourth-order integrator takes steps of at most one second and 0.02 radians of stage rotation, splitting exactly at release. A detected fault is refined within its step.</p><p>Automated checks cover analytic mass moments, independent force and torque balances, conservation, release continuity, free cargo motion, step and quadrature refinement, and limit events. Frozen Earth constants match the <a href="/lab/method/">Earth experiment</a>. These checks validate this approximation, not the omitted hardware.</p></section>
